//	xtree: std::_Tree, the balanced tree behind std::map, std::multimap, std::set and std::multiset.
// This project's own reconstruction of the original's tree from the executable's code; the units
// find it first because op/ leads the include path, so `#include <xtree>` from <map> and <set> reaches it.
// Evidence: the constructor 0x0042b346 (_Init), the destructors 0x005fb033 and 0x004c6dc1, _Lbound
// 0x004793a8, _Buynode 0x00613bc0, _Lrotate 0x00613b70, _Inc 0x00613c70, _Dec 0x006d40d0, _Min
// 0x0070dc20, _Copy 0x00679310, equal_range 0x00636e70, and the decorated names in the symbol table
// (class, template parameters, nested _Node, iterator and const_iterator, member names, argument types).
// Layout: the allocator at +0 and the key compare at +1 (both empty), _Head +4, _Nil +8, _Multi +0xc,
// _Size +0x10 (0x14 bytes). Each tree owns its nil node, made in _Init and freed in the destructor,
// so there are no static _Nil or _Nilrefs data. A node is _Left +0, _Parent +4, _Right +8, the value,
// then a colour byte and a nil-flag byte; _Buynode takes the colour as a char. The routines that
// cannot reach an object (_Min, _Max, the iterators' _Inc and _Dec) stop at the node's nil flag.
// Some members are defined outside the class on purpose: the original calls them out of line.

